Abstract
Small multirotor UAVs offer precise hover and manoeuvrability but remain limited by short flight endurance. This project investigates the use of helium-assisted buoyancy to reduce lift demand and improve hover endurance in a lightweight multirotor platform. The study combines analytical modelling, ROS 2 simulation, and experimental testing to evaluate a buoyancy-assisted UAV design.
A MATLAB-based envelope analysis compared candidate balloon geometries using surface area to volume ratio, spatial efficiency, and disturbance response. The spherical geometry was identified as the most suitable configuration, providing the strongest overall performance across these criteria. ROS 2 simulation was then used to assess duty-cycled thrust as a control strategy for buoyancy-assisted hover. Experimental testing included physical balloon lift measurement and comparative hover endurance tests using a Crazyflie 2.1+ baseline platform.
