Initial findings show that helium-assisted buoyancy increases hover endurance relative to the unmodified baseline system. Final quantitative results are still being refined through repeated trials and statistical analysis. Overall, the project demonstrates the potential of buoyancy-assisted lift to improve small UAV endurance and establishes a reproducible framework for future design, control, and validation of hybrid buoyant multirotor systems.
1. Introduction
Commercial multirotor UAVs typically achieve flight durations of 20–40 minutes under load. Application-specific systems such as DJI’s Agras series operate for approximately 15 minutes despite high power input (UAV Systems International, 2025; DJI, 2025).
Small multirotor UAVs offer precise hover and manoeuvrability. They have limited endurance. Motors must generate lift continuously to balance weight (UAV Systems International, 2025). This produces high power draw during hover and low-speed flight.
This project evaluates whether helium-assisted buoyancy can reduce lift demand and extend flight time. The design adds a helium envelope to a lightweight rotorcraft so that part of the vehicle weight is supported passively. The propulsion system then supplies less lift during sustained flight.
Prior work shows that buoyancy-assisted aerial vehicles can extend endurance. It also shows that design trade-offs are not fully resolved for small hybrid systems. Existing studies discuss envelope geometry, buoyancy sizing, connector design, and flight-time improvement. Many do not justify each design choice with sufficient technical depth or link geometry selection to performance evaluation in a reproducible way (Macias and Lee, 2022).
This project uses the literature to inform method. The review supports four design decisions. First, endurance is evaluated against a conventional rotorcraft baseline. Second, envelope shape is selected using explicit geometric and performance criteria. Third, buoyancy is assessed against both lift benefit and added system mass. Fourth, testing focuses on hover and low-speed operation, where buoyancy most directly offsets motor effort (Palossi et al., 2017; Macias and Lee, 2022).
This study is scoped to indoor operation. It assumes low air disturbance, with minor perturbations from HVAC or local airflow. This constrains experimental conditions and limits applicability to outdoor or turbulent environments.
2. Literature Review
2.1 Endurance Constraint in Multirotor UAVs
Multirotor UAVs consume substantial power because they must produce lift continuously. This limits flight time and constrains persistent missions such as inspection and monitoring (Palossi et al., 2017).
Energy modelling studies show that predicted endurance varies significantly with modelling assumptions and platform configuration. This variability reduces the reliability of purely theoretical approaches and supports experimental baseline comparison (Zhang et al.).
Helium assistance is only meaningful if it reduces required thrust. A direct comparison between a standard UAV and a buoyancy-assisted configuration provides a measurable evaluation of this effect.
2.2 Helium-Assisted Hybrid UAVs as a Relevant Design Path}
Macias and Lee (2022) present a helium-assisted hybrid drone designed to increase flight time while maintaining compactness. Their work addresses envelope geometry, buoyant force, and indoor flight testing.
Their platform uses a GEPRC Rocket Lite quadcopter with a 1300 mAh 4-cell Li-Po battery at 14.8 V. The drone and battery weigh approximately 315 g. The hybrid system mass increases to 347.9 g after structural integration. The balloon provides an estimated lifting force of 133 g, reducing the effective supported weight to 214.9 g. Experimental indoor flight time exceeds 46 minutes compared to approximately 18 minutes for the original system, corresponding to an improvement greater than 2.5× (Macias and Lee, 2022).
Palossi et al. (2017) demonstrate a 52 g buoyancy-assisted indoor platform capable of sustained hover at approximately 200 mW. With solar integration, the system achieves flight durations ranging from tens to hundreds of hours under controlled conditions.
The BlimpleBee platform achieves stable indoor autonomous flight at approximately 5 W. The system has a total mass of 490 g, including a 110 g payload, and operates for approximately 30 minutes using a 3S 1000 mAh Li-Po battery (Hickson et al.).
These systems demonstrate that buoyancy-assisted flight performance depends strongly on system mass, payload, onboard computation, and control complexity. Endurance improvements must therefore be interpreted within the context of total system design.
2.3 Envelope Geometry and Shape Selection
Envelope geometry determines helium volume, buoyant lift, and spatial constraints.
Macias and Lee (2022) compare cuboid and spherical envelopes under fixed dimensional constraints of 0.75 × 0.75 × 0.55 m. They evaluate shape performance using volume and lifting-force-to-characteristic-height ratio. Under these constraints, the cuboid provides greater volume and improved lift characteristics relative to the sphere, leading to its selection.
This comparison uses arbitrary constraints by forcing geometric equivalence between shapes, limiting volumetric optimisation.
A more rigorous approach defines geometry from required buoyant lift. A target buoyancy ratio and system mass determine required helium volume. Each candidate shape can then be dimensioned from this volume. This enables direct comparison of geometric efficiency across shapes per required volume without imposed dimensional constraints.
2.4 Duty-Cycled Thrust and Control Strategy
Palossi et al. (2017) show that once buoyancy offsets a portion of system weight, continuous thrust is not required. Duty-cycled actuation reduces average power consumption during hover.
Lee et al. (IEEE, 2024) analyse instability caused by separation between the centre of buoyancy and centre of mass, referred to as the reverse pendulum effect. They implement adaptive control to maintain stability under this configuration.
Macias and Lee (2022) address stability through mechanical design. They demonstrate that a ball-and-socket joint improves controllability relative to a fixed connection.
This project does not analyse mechanical joint design. A connection type is selected based on prior work and is outside the scope of this report. Stability is managed through control strategies, including duty-cycled thrust activated using system feedback. This approach links stability control and energy reduction within a single framework under indoor operating conditions.
2.5 Methodological Synthesis and Research Gap
The literature supports a structured methodological approach.
Baseline comparison provides a reliable measure of endurance improvement due to variability in modelling approaches (Zhang et al.; Macias and Lee, 2022).
Envelope geometry should be derived from required buoyant lift rather than constrained geometric assumptions (Macias and Lee, 2022).
System mass must be included in performance evaluation, as structural and envelope mass reduce net buoyancy benefit (Macias and Lee, 2022).
Hover and low-speed operation are the most relevant conditions for evaluating buoyancy-assisted systems (Palossi et al., 2017).
Control strategies influence both stability and energy consumption, linking performance and efficiency in buoyancy-assisted flight (Palossi et al., 2017; Lee et al., 2024).
Despite demonstrated endurance improvements, the literature does not provide a consistent framework for small-scale hybrid UAV design. Shape selection is often constrained rather than derived. Stability and control are treated separately rather than as a unified system. Many studies lack sufficient technical justification for design decisions.
This project addresses these gaps by deriving envelope geometry from buoyancy requirements, evaluating geometric efficiency across shapes, and testing performance using controlled indoor baseline comparison. It integrates control-based stability and energy reduction within this framework.
3. Methodology
3.1 Methodological Approach
This study examines the impact of helium-assisted buoyancy on multirotor UAV endurance through analytical, simulation, and experimental methods. The methodology addresses limitations identified in the literature, especially the lack of a consistent framework that links envelope design, buoyancy performance, and endurance validation (Macias and Lee, 2022; Zhang et al.).
The work is divided into three connected stages. First, envelope geometry is derived analytically and evaluated computationally to determine the relationship between shape, volume, and buoyant lift. Second, control strategies are assessed through simulation to investigate how reduced effective weight influences thrust demand and enables duty-cycled operation. Third, a physical prototype is used to experimentally validate both buoyancy predictions and endurance improvements using a controlled baseline comparison.
All testing is conducted indoors under low-disturbance conditions. This assumption matches the indoor operating conditions used in previous buoyancy-assisted UAV studies and helps isolate the effects of buoyancy and control from environmental variability (Macias and Lee, 2022; Palossi et al., 2017).
