3.2.1 Envelope Geometry and Buoyancy Determination
The design of the helium envelope is based on a buoyancy-driven approach rather than fixed geometric constraints. The required helium volume is determined from the target system mass and a defined buoyancy ratio. This links geometry selection to functional performance rather than imposed dimensional assumptions. This approach was chosen because previous work often fixed geometry before evaluating buoyancy performance, which limits comparison across shapes (Macias and Lee, 2022).
Buoyant lift is calculated using the difference in density between air and helium. The governing relationship is defined as:
F_b = (ρ_air − ρ_He) · V · g
Where ρ_air and ρ_He represent the densities of air and helium respectively, V is the helium volume, and g is gravitational acceleration. For practical comparison with system mass, lift is expressed in grams.
A target buoyancy ratio (β) is defined to represent the proportion of system weight offset by helium:
β = L / m_system
The values used in this study are not fixed at this stage. Instead, the model is implemented through a user-defined interface that allows both system mass and target buoyancy ratio to be specified as input parameters.
