3.3.2 UAV Endurance Testing
UAV endurance testing uses a controlled comparative experiment with a Crazyflie 2.1+ platform. The purpose of this stage is to quantify the effect of helium-assisted buoyancy on flight time relative to an unmodified baseline configuration. A baseline comparison is necessary because prior energy modelling studies show that endurance predictions vary significantly with modelling assumptions and system configuration (Zhang et al.).
Two test conditions are evaluated: a baseline configuration and a buoyancy-assisted configuration. In the baseline condition, the Crazyflie 2.1+ is flown in hover under controlled indoor conditions until battery depletion, and the flight time is recorded. In the buoyancy-assisted condition, the same platform is fitted with a helium balloon designed to provide a known lift force, and the hover test is repeated under the same operating conditions. The attachment method is fixed and selected based on prior work. It is not varied within this study. This decision follows the study scope set by the literature. Attachment design is recognised as important, but it is excluded here so the study can isolate the effects of buoyancy level and control strategy (Macias and Lee, 2022; Lee et al., 2024).
All endurance tests are conducted indoors under consistent conditions. Battery state, UAV hardware configuration, and test environment are controlled so that the primary difference between the two conditions is the presence of buoyant lift.
Each condition is tested over five trials. This repeated-measures approach reduces the influence of experimental noise and improves confidence that observed changes in flight time reflect overall system behaviour. Repeated controlled comparison is consistent with the methodological need for empirical validation identified in prior comparative endurance studies (Macias and Lee, 2022; Zhang et al.).
